{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And if your brother is not near you, etc., and it shall be with you until your brother seeks it.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Bava Metzia 27a): 'Seek it from him — if he is a deceiver.' And in any case, 'it' refers to the ox or sheep, for it should have said 'until your brother seeks it and comes for it,' but rather that your brother comes and seeks it through inquiry and investigation — and that is by means of identifying marks — until it becomes clear to you that he is truly seeking this particular ox and no other. And from this we infer that one must investigate even if he is not a deceiver. But the verse does not depart from its plain sense, as the Ra'am explained, that 'it' refers to your brother. And what is written, 'you may not remain indifferent,' after it already says 'and you find it,' which conveys that it has come into your hand (ibid. 27a), is because the matter is given over to the heart: for if your brother comes and seeks it and says, 'An ox with such-and-such a mark is lost to me,' and your inclination seduces you to turn a blind eye and to rule permissively for yourself — perhaps he saw it in someone else's possession, or you wish to cast aspersion on his identifying marks in order to exempt yourself from it in human courts — even so, you may not remain indifferent because of the Eye from above, which sees and recognizes your stratagems."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:13:11.885000261Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}